Bipartite Graphs

1.Intro to bipartite graphs, how to detect them using 2-coloring, and their use in problems like team assignment and scheduling conflicts.
2.A bipartite graph is one where nodes can be divided into two groups such that every edge goes between the groups and none within a group. Real-world example: candidates ↔ companies. Graphs with odd cycles (like a triangle) are not bipartite.
3.A graph is bipartite iff it can be 2-colored so that no edge connects nodes of the same color; the two colors directly represent the two groups.
4.In a bipartite graph, vertices can be divided into two sets so every edge goes across the sets; this division may vary in disconnected graphs, but in a connected graph choosing one node’s side fixes all others.
5.Connected graph ⇒ paths force colors uniquely; disconnected graph ⇒ no paths, so free choice.
6.In a bipartite graph, nodes are divided into two partitions (U and V) such that every edge goes between the two.The partitioning is not unique for disconnected graphs, but in a connected bipartite graph, once you fix one node’s side, the positions of all other nodes are forced by the graph structure.
7.The two groups in a bipartite graph are called partitions.
8.The red nodes form one group, blue nodes form the other. This is why bipartite graphs are also called "2-colorable."
9.Bipartite graphs model two-sided relationships
--->people and course:edges connect people to courses they are enrolled in.
--->Job applicants and positions: edges show who's qualified for what.
--->people and products: edges represent purchases or reviews.
--->Authors and papers: edges link authors to their publications.
10.Because edges connect only across the two types, the graph is bipartite, which enables efficient matching and assignment algorithms that are not applicable to general graphs.
11.A graph is bipartite iff it has no odd-length cycle. Odd cycles (like a triangle) break 2-coloring because colors conflict when the cycle closes, while even cycles work since colors alternate and match up correctly.
12.When you 2-color a cycle, odd-length cycles force a conflict (start and end get the same color), while even-length cycles alternate cleanly with no conflict, explaining why odd cycles make a graph non-bipartite.







